// Formative practice quiz for big-o-basics. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design.
$CATEGORY: collections-streams/collections-in-depth/big-o-basics

::big-o-basics o1 - contains on an ArrayList::What cost does the Java API documentation give for contains on an ArrayList of n elements?{
=Linear, O(n)#Correct. contains is one of the operations the ArrayList documentation says run in linear time; it checks elements one by one.
~Constant, O(1)#Not quite. Constant time applies to get by index, not to searching by value.
~Logarithmic, O(log n)#Not quite. An ArrayList is not a search tree; it scans.
~Quadratic, O(n^2)#Not quite. One contains call is a single scan, not every pair.
}

::big-o-basics o1 - TreeMap versus HashMap lookup::Which statement about get matches the Java API documentation?{
=TreeMap.get is guaranteed O(log n); HashMap.get is O(1) when hash codes spread well#Correct. TreeMap guarantees log(n) for get; HashMap gives constant time under a good hash spread.
~Both are guaranteed O(1)#Not quite. TreeMap walks a sorted tree, so its lookups cost log(n).
~Both are O(n), because they look at every entry#Not quite. Neither scans every entry in normal use.
~HashMap.get is O(log n) and TreeMap.get is O(1)#Not quite. That swaps the two.
}

::big-o-basics o2 - doubling a linear loop::A method loops once over a list and takes 40 ms on 50,000 elements. About how long on 100,000?{
=About 80 ms#Correct. One pass is O(n), so doubling n roughly doubles the time.
~About 40 ms#Not quite. That would be constant time; the loop visits every element.
~About 160 ms#Not quite. Quadrupling is the pattern of nested loops, O(n^2).
~About 41 ms#Not quite. Growing by one step is the logarithmic pattern.
}

::big-o-basics o2 - doubling a constant operation::Reading arrayList.get(500) takes a tiny, fixed time. What happens to that time when the list grows from 1,000 to 2,000 elements?{
=It stays about the same#Correct. get by index is constant time\: it reads one slot whatever the size.
~It doubles#Not quite. Doubling is the pattern of a full pass, not of one indexed read.
~It quadruples#Not quite. That is the pattern of comparing every pair.
~It halves#Not quite. More elements never make a single read faster.
}

::big-o-basics o3 - a hidden loop::Code loops over n orders and, inside the loop, calls blocked.contains(id), where blocked is an ArrayList of m IDs. What is the complexity, and what fixes it?{
=O(n * m); copy blocked into a HashSet once before the loop, making it O(n + m)#Correct. list.contains is itself a loop, and a HashSet lookup is O(1) expected.
~O(n); it is already one loop#Not quite. The contains call hides a second loop over m IDs.
~O(n * m); build a new HashSet of blocked inside the loop#Not quite. Building the set on every pass still costs O(m) each time.
~O(log n); sort orders first#Not quite. Sorting orders does not remove the scan of blocked.
}

::big-o-basics o3 - finding a repeated value::To check whether a list of n strings has a repeat, a student adds each string to a HashSet and stops when add returns false. What is the expected complexity?{
=O(n)#Correct. One pass, with an O(1) expected add for each string.
~O(n^2)#Not quite. That is the cost of comparing every pair, which the set avoids.
~O(1)#Not quite. Each string must still be added once, so the work grows with n.
~O(log n)#Not quite. The pass visits every string; nothing halves the work.
}
